WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
Why does a cardinal stay when hummingbirds leave for winter?

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.280
Use a card sort and a window watch to explore that with your child.

3
00:00:11.383 --> 00:00:16.303
Your child practices sorting with reasons and changing a guess when new evidence

4
00:00:16.303 --> 00:00:20.603
comes in. They also count what they see and compare seasons.

5
00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:25.603
This takes about thirty minutes, plus a fifteen-minute window watch.

6
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:29.537
Draw, print, or cut out ten animal cards.

7
00:00:30.937 --> 00:00:32.157
Label them with these names.

8
00:00:33.537 --> 00:00:38.297
Ruby-throated hummingbird, monarch butterfly, tundra swan, groundhog, and bat.

9
00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:44.397
Eastern box turtle, wood frog, white-tailed deer, gray squirrel, and northern

10
00:00:44.397 --> 00:00:45.397
cardinal.

11
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.650
Lay out the three sheets: Travel, Rest, and Stay and change.

12
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.010
Let your child sort the cards with no help.

13
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.370
Ask, 'How did you decide?'

14
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.590
Write the reasons down, and don't correct anything yet.

15
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:01.357
Now a question for you.

16
00:01:01.357 --> 00:01:06.057
Hummingbirds leave Virginia in fall, but cardinals stay all year.

17
00:01:06.797 --> 00:01:09.097
What do you predict is the main reason?

18
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.270
Cold matters, but food is a big reason.

19
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.790
Hummingbirds eat nectar and insects, and those run out in a Virginia winter.

20
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.770
So they travel to where food is.

21
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:30.230
Cardinals stay all year and fluff their feathers in the cold.

22
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:34.663
Use the guide’s animal list to check each card together.

23
00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:37.603
Ask, 'Where do you think this one goes?'

24
00:01:38.203 --> 00:01:39.463
Let your child move cards.

25
00:01:40.803 --> 00:01:42.503
Groundhogs hibernate in burrows.

26
00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:44.903
Deer grow a winter coat.

27
00:01:46.343 --> 00:01:47.963
Gray squirrels dig up buried nuts.

28
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:52.127
When a card moves, ask, 'What made you change your mind?'

29
00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:54.527
Tundra swans are a fun surprise.

30
00:01:55.907 --> 00:02:00.487
They nest in the Arctic and fly thousands of miles south to spend winter here.

31
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.550
Sit at the window for fifteen minutes.

32
00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.910
Have your child predict how many kinds of birds you will see.

33
00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.130
Write down their guess.

34
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.250
Tally the birds of each kind and note what they do.

35
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.310
An empty yard is a result too.

36
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:20.673
Stay with your child for the whole activity.

37
00:02:22.053 --> 00:02:25.053
Nobody touches a wild animal, alive or dead.

38
00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:29.453
Never touch a bat, even one that seems asleep or hurt.

39
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.013
Bats can carry rabies.

40
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:36.807
Leave resting turtles and frogs covered where you find them.

41
00:02:37.747 --> 00:02:39.307
Use a feeder only in winter.

42
00:02:40.607 --> 00:02:44.427
An adult handles it and washes hands after touching it or the seed.

43
00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.887
Keep birdseed away from children under four.

44
00:02:49.087 --> 00:02:51.327
If a child is choking, call nine one one.

45
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.580
Repeat the window watch in another season, at the same time of day.

46
00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:58.960
Compare the two sheets.

47
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.800
Which kinds did you see both times?

48
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.420
Which did you see only once?

49
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.120
Think about what food was available in each season.
